xiii)Provide fpr qroup insurance or additional insurance coverage for baranqay officials, incfudinq members of barangay tanod brigades and other service units with public or private insurance companies, when the finances of the municipal government allow said coverage. " (Emphasis Supplied)

"SECTION 458. Powers, Duties, Functions and Compensation.— (a)The sangguniang panlungsod, as the legislative body of the city, shall enact ordinances, approve resolutions and appropriate funds for the general welfare of the city and its inhabitants pursuant to Section 16 of this Code and in the proper exercise of the corporate powers of the city as provided for under Section 22 of this Code, and shall: (T)Approve ordinances and pass resolutions necessary for an efficient and effective city government and in this connection shall: xxx xxx xxx (xiii)Provide for group insurance or additional insurance coveraqe for all baranqay officials, including members of baranqay tanod briqades and other service units, with public or private insurance companies, when the finances of the city qovernment allow said coverage." (Emphasis Supplied) Thereafter, the Government Service Insurance4System (GSIS) issued Opinion No. 09-2000 dated 11 April 2000, which, in substance, declared that barangay officials cannot procure pension plans and other inSurance coverage from private insurance companies. Thru DILG Opinion No. 80, series of 2000, this Department disagreed with the aforesaid Opinion of the GSIS and sought the favor of the Commission on Audit (COA) to reconsider its position in adopting the said GSIS Opinion on the following groundst to wit: i 2. 3. The exclusive mandate of the GSIS to provide the pension and insurance needs of government officers and employees does not extend to barangay officials. Barangay officials and employees deserve to be treated as a class by themselves considering the form of their compensation for services rendered. For the purpose, they deserve more in terms of social legislation than other officials and employees in the higher level of governance; The legislative intent of providing barangay officials and employees ample protection as a class is thus even bolstered by applying the rule of statutory construction. The insurance coverage of barangay officials under Sections 447 and 458 of the Local Government Code of 1991 may be procured from public or private insurance companies should be interpreted as an insurance coverage on top of the insurance benefits that shalt be enjoyed by the barangay officials and employees under the insurance system that will be established by the GSIS under Section 522 of the same Code. However, Sections 447 and 458 provide that the sangguniang bayan/panlungsod shall approve ordinances and pass resolutions necessary for an efficient and effective municipal/city government; This Department had already the occasion to rule otherwise on a similar query (i.e. DILG Opinion No. I IO-A, series of 1999; DILG Opinion No. 83, series of 1998);

4. 5. The GSIS Opinion should be properly corrected as it would only sow confusion since many municipalities and cities nationwide had been contracting private insurance for its barangay officials, especially barangay tanods, invoking the previous opinion of this Department; and Barangay governments cannot directly procure additional insurance coverage for the punong barangays, sangguniang barangay members, the barangay secretary and the barangay treasurer for lack of legal basis to do so. Meanwhile, in a letter dated 08 September 2011 addressed to you, which was attached to your instant letter, the GSIS stated and we quote: "In view of the continuous non-remittance by the DBM of the premium contributions due the GSIS, the payment of claims and other benefits for Barangay and Sanggunian Officials covered under RA 6942 was temporarily suspended pursuant to GSIS Board Resolution No. 332 dated November 27, 2001. The suspension shall remain in force until such time that the said premiums due have been remitted to the GSIS. " (Emphasis Supplied) Thus, while it is true that barangay governm&nts cannot directly procure pension plans and other insurance coverage from private insurance companies, however, given the non-remittance by the DBM of bihe premium contributions due the GSIS and its possible repercussion to the present conditions and welt-being of the barangay officials, we are of the view that such procurement may be permitted at this point in time. It is well-settled that the law does not require that the impossible be done. The law obliges no one to perform an impossibility, expressed in the maxim, nemo tenetur ad impossible. In other words, there is no obligation to do an impossible thing. Impossibilium nulla obligato est. Hence, the Local Government Code or the RA 6942 may not be so construed as to require compliance with what it prescribes cannot, at the time, be legally done. Coincidentally, it must be presumed that the legislature did not at all intend an interpretation or application of a law which is far removed from the realm of the possible. Truly, in the interpretation of statutes, the interpretation to be given must be such that it is in accordance with logic, common sense, reasonableness and practicality% After all, the provision of insurance benefits to barangay official is a social legislation. We hope we have addressed your concern accordingly. Very truly yours, JESUS B. Dire Ls/17 O, Copy furnished: RD BLANDINO M. MACEDA DILG-Region V Rizal St., Legazpi City or Ill
